  </p>

  <h2 id="iti">When ITI or vocational training makes sense after 10th</h2>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">ITI fits when</div>
      <ul>
        <li>You learn better by doing than by sitting through abstract theory for years.</li>
        <li>You are open to serious trade skill, apprenticeships, workshop discipline, and steady mastery.</li>
        <li>You are willing to add communication and digital skill on top of practical training.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">ITI becomes weak when</div>
      <ul>
        <li>It is treated like a punishment route instead of a professional route.</li>
        <li>You stop at the minimum level and never stack tools, visibility, or better opportunities later.</li>
        <li>You enter without checking the trade quality, seriousness, or progression path.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Practical routes can become powerful.
    </p>
    <p>
      But only if the student treats them as a long-term skill asset, not as a dead-end label.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="iti-trades">Specific ITI trades in high demand right now</h2>

  <p>
    India has over 14,000 ITIs (about 2,200 government and 12,100 private).
    Most students know ITI exists — few know which trades actually have demand.
    Here is what the market is paying attention to right now.
  </p>

  <div class="grid-two">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>High-demand ITI trades and their entry salary</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Electrician:</strong> One of the most consistently in-demand trades. Entry salary ₹1.5–3 LPA; master electricians in industrial work earn ₹4–8 LPA. High EV infrastructure and solar demand is adding new sub-roles.</li>
        <li><strong>Welder / Fabricator:</strong> Strong demand in shipbuilding, automotive, and construction. Entry ₹1.5–2.5 LPA; senior welders in aerospace or specialty work earn ₹5–10 LPA.</li>
        <li><strong>COPA (Computer Operator and Programming Assistant):</strong> Entry into IT support, data entry, and office administration. Useful for ITI students who want to layer digital skills on top.</li>
        <li><strong>Mechanic (Motor Vehicle / Diesel):</strong> Steady demand from auto industry and fleet operators. EV mechanic roles are a newer high-demand variant beginning to appear.</li>
        <li><strong>Fitter:</strong> Core manufacturing trade. Used in production lines, engineering firms, and defence-linked PSUs. Entry ₹1.8–3 LPA; grows with seniority and sector.</li>
        <li><strong>Plumber:</strong> Acute shortage in urban infrastructure. Entry ₹1.5–2.5 LPA; self-employed experienced plumbers in metros earn ₹5–12 LPA.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>What you can do after ITI besides a direct job</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Apprenticeship India:</strong> The government apprenticeship portal lets ITI graduates earn while they train at companies across sectors. This can significantly raise both skill and starting salary.</li>
        <li><strong>Lateral entry into diploma:</strong> Several polytechnic institutes offer second-year lateral entry for ITI graduates, giving you a Diploma in Engineering without starting from scratch.</li>
        <li><strong>Government exam routes:</strong> Railway, defence, and SSC exams have specific openings for 10th-pass and ITI-qualified students in technical support and maintenance roles.</li>
        <li><strong>Self-employment:</strong> Electricians, plumbers, AC technicians, and motor mechanics with good service track records can build substantial local self-employment income within 3–5 years.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    The key rule for ITI routes: the trade matters less than your willingness to keep layering skills, communication, and digital fluency on top of the practical base. The initial earning gap vs. traditional school routes is real, but the ceiling is not as low as most families assume.
  </p>

